  "taxTreatment": {
    "consequencesStated": [
      {
        "text": "A net contribution of \u00A380 effectively becomes \u00A3100 gross in your pension.",
        "topic": "PensionTaxRelief",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "linkedObjectiveIds": [
          "OBJ5"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            12
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "You will receive 20% tax relief on contributions, meaning the government adds \u00A3720 to your \u00A32,880 net contribution.",
        "topic": "PensionTaxReliefAmount",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "linkedObjectiveIds": [
          "OBJ5"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            13
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "If you contribute more than you earn in this tax year, you\u0027ll face a tax charge because you\u0027ll have received tax relief beyond your income limit. In most cases, HMRC will treat the excess as additional income for the year, and you\u0027ll be taxed on it at your marginal rate.",
        "topic": "PensionContributionExcessTax",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            12
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Investment gains within an ISA are tax free. There is no tax to pay on any interest or dividends earned within a cash ISA or Innovative Finance ISA, and there is no Income Tax or Capital Gains Tax to pay on profits made within a stocks and shares ISA.",
        "topic": "ISATaxFreeGains",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            31
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "By using a GIA, you can take advantage of your annual Capital Gains Tax (CGT) allowance through careful planning, helping to minimise tax on your investment gains.",
        "topic": "GIACGTPlanning",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "linkedObjectiveIds": [
          "OBJ2"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            14
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "The recommended Bed \u0026 ISA facility allows you to gradually transfer investments into an ISA/Pension each tax year, making your portfolio as tax-efficient as possible over time.",
        "topic": "BedAndISATaxPlanning",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "linkedObjectiveIds": [
          "OBJ2"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            14
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Should you die before age 75, the nominated beneficiaries can receive a lump sum, drawdown an income or purchase a dependents annuity. All these options are paid free of tax. Where you die after the age of 75, the same options are available, but tax is paid at the beneficiary\u0027s marginal rate of tax.",
        "topic": "PensionDeathBenefitTaxTreatment",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            12
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "An ISA does not lose its tax-free status on the death of the investor. It becomes a \u0027continuing ISA\u0027 and will remain so for up to three years, until the account is closed, or until the administration of the deceased\u0027s estate is completed, whichever comes first.",
        "topic": "ISADeathTaxTreatment",
        "isClientSpecific": false,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            31
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "Historically, pensions have not formed part of your estate for IHT purposes, making them a valuable estate planning tool. However, legislation has now been passed to change this.",
        "topic": "PensionIHTChange",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            4
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice"
        }
      },
      {
        "text": "From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT. Exceptions include pensions paid to a surviving spouse or civil partner, dependents\u0027 scheme pensions, and death-in-service benefits.",
        "topic": "PensionIHTInclusionFromApril2027",
        "isClientSpecific": true,
        "provenance": {
          "assertionStatus": "Stated",
          "pageNumbers": [
            4,
            12
          ],
          "documentCategory": "I_RecommendationsAndAdvice",
          "quote": "From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT."
        }
      }
    ],
